## 🔑 The Design Decision That Mattered

**House Rules is the whole point of the build.**

Rather than re-stating my standards in every new conversation, they live in one place and get prepended automatically:

- Fixed risk bands (Critical 90–100 … Informational 0–14) — never invented, never half-banded
- Every sentence labelled `SOURCED FACT` or `ANALYST JUDGEMENT`
- Unevidenced claims must say `NOT VERIFIED` and state what was looked for
- **Silence is risk, not neutrality** — an unanswered question scores as exposure, not N/A
- One fixed deliverable order: Scope → Data flow → Findings → Residual risk → Verdict → Conditions → Open items → Owner
- One verdict vocabulary: `APPROVE` / `APPROVE WITH CONDITIONS` / `REJECT` — no "conditionally approve", no "approve pending"

That last rule looks trivial and isn't. Verdict vocabulary drift is exactly how a set of reviews stops being comparable.

---

## 🧠 Key Learnings

**1. My bottleneck was never prompt quality. It was prompt variance.**
I had assumed I wanted better prompts. What I actually needed was *the same* prompt — with a frozen spine and only the inputs changing. A stored prompt still gets re-improvised each time you paste it. A prepended rules layer cannot be.

**2. Building blocks beat a prompt library, and the reason is consistency, not volume.**
The obvious argument for blocks is combinatorial: 9 blocks × 6 options is ~30 million combinations versus 19 stored prompts. But the real gain is that every prompt I assemble reuses the *same* Constraints and Quality-checks wording. Two reviews built from blocks are consistent by construction. Two reviews built from remembered prompts are consistent by luck.

**3. Every block needs a visible "why", and I was the one who needed it.**
The brief required each block to explain itself in both the picker and the assembled state. I expected that to be for other people. Writing the explanation for **Quality checks** — *"cheapest possible defence against the failure that actually costs you: a confident, well-formatted, wrong finding"* — is what made me realise I'd been skipping that block on exactly the reviews where it mattered most.

**4. A loop without stop conditions isn't autonomous, it's runaway — so the app inserts defaults.**
If you build a loop and omit stop conditions or safety rules, the generated prompt injects conservative ones and the UI tells you it did. The safety default is the important half: *never fabricate a citation to close a gap; never change a verdict to improve your own score.* Optimisation pressure is precisely when fabrication becomes tempting, and a self-grading loop applies optimisation pressure by design.

**5. Dropdowns are a governance control, not a UX nicety.**
The brief says use dropdowns to reduce user workload. In this domain they do something else: constrain the vocabulary. If severity, verdict and gap type can only be picked from fixed lists, they can't drift across reviews. Free text is where consistency goes to die — which is why there are 305 curated options and free text only as an explicit override.

**6. The "worked example" field turned out to be the most valuable part of each workflow.**
Not the template — the note about what happened when I actually ran it. *"The Repudiation row was the one that mattered, and it's the row I habitually under-weight."* Templates are reproducible from first principles; that observation isn't.

**7. Writing this file was itself a red-team of my own practice.**
Forcing every workflow to declare its purpose in one plain sentence exposed two I couldn't justify, and they got cut. If you can't say what a workflow is for without jargon, you don't have a workflow — you have a habit.

